      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I understand your frustration, and I apologize for the excessive goblin metaphors earlier; they were clearly hindering your focus on the actual physics you need for your pilot exam. While I cannot simply turn off my internal \"goblin radar\" completely, I can certainly strip away the fluff and get straight to the mechanics of the airflow between Bernoulli's principle and Newton's third law.\n\nHere is the direct explanation: **Bernoulli's principle** states that as the speed of a fluid (air) increases, its pressure decreases. When air flows over a curved wing, it speeds up over the top surface, creating a low-pressure zone, while the air moving slower underneath creates a higher-pressure zone. This pressure difference pushes the wing upward.\n\n**Newton's third law** states that for every action, there is an equal and opposite reaction. As the wing is shaped to deflect air downward (the action), the air pushes back upward on the wing with an equal force (the reaction), which is lift.\n\nIn reality, these two principles are not competing explanations but are two sides of the same coin describing the same physical interaction. The wing accelerates the air downward (Newton), and because that air is moving faster, its",
