    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, absolutely! Increasing the angle of attack (tilting the nose up) generally increases the downwash, which strengthens the reaction force from Newton's third law and boosts lift—up to a point. However, if you tilt the nose up too much, the airflow can separate from the wing's surface, causing a stall where the lift drops dramatically.\n\nThink of it like a goblin trying to push a heavy boulder: a gentle push (small angle of attack) moves it slowly, but a strong, coordinated shove (higher angle of attack) moves it faster. If the goblin loses their footing or the boulder gets too slippery (stall), the push becomes useless.\n\nIn your exam, remember that while a higher angle of attack increases lift, it also increases drag and can lead to a stall if exceeded. This balance is crucial for safe flight maneuvers.\n\nJust like a goblin might try to run faster to catch a train but ends up falling into a puddle if they don't watch their step, pushing the angle of attack too high can cause the wing to lose its ability to generate lift. So, while tilting the nose up helps with lift, you need to manage it carefully to avoid a stall.",
